00:09:53,620 --> 00:09:54,940
Nothing, they don't want you to have anything

263
00:09:54,940 --> 00:09:56,340
to do with education.

264
00:09:56,340 --> 00:09:58,820
But, so you can sit there, you can go and read,

265
00:09:58,820 --> 00:10:03,080
you can write a novel, which was a key plot point

266
00:10:03,080 --> 00:10:05,820
in the show, The Affair, if you ever saw that.

267
00:10:05,820 --> 00:10:07,140
This actually, the whole thing was,

268
00:10:07,140 --> 00:10:09,060
they had the rubber room, which is, anyway.

269
00:10:09,060 --> 00:10:11,340
Then eventually, there was an expose news article on this,

270
00:10:11,340 --> 00:10:13,540
and people got really mad about this.

271
00:10:13,540 --> 00:10:15,620
And now, what does the New York system do?

272
00:10:15,620 --> 00:10:16,980
New York public school system,

273
00:10:16,980 --> 00:10:19,940
now they divide them among a number of smaller rooms,

274
00:10:19,940 --> 00:10:21,620
which are not as newsworthy.

275
00:10:21,620 --> 00:10:26,100
It was, but they had one room where 150 Cretans go,

276
00:10:26,100 --> 00:10:28,860
and that was like, this room was full of 150

277
00:10:28,860 --> 00:10:32,140
of the worst teachers in New York, who get paid,

278
00:10:32,140 --> 00:10:34,860
I think they get like $100,000 a year, to do nothing.

279
00:10:35,740 --> 00:10:36,660
All right, and that didn't go over well.

280
00:10:36,660 --> 00:10:39,900
So now, there's more rooms, same basic strategy.

281
00:10:39,900 --> 00:10:41,500
Anyway, so in the Whitman world,

282
00:10:41,500 --> 00:10:43,220
you would actually come up with some way

283
00:10:43,220 --> 00:10:44,900
of bartering them out of it.

284
00:10:44,900 --> 00:10:46,980
And then, like, how would you go

285
00:10:46,980 --> 00:10:48,020
and avoid the other problems?

286
00:10:48,020 --> 00:10:48,900
Well, probably what you'd do is,

287
00:10:48,900 --> 00:10:50,380
you would just go to the teachers union,

288
00:10:50,380 --> 00:10:51,660
with a big bag of money, and say,

289
00:10:51,660 --> 00:10:53,460
here's the bag of money, so that we can start firing

290
00:10:53,460 --> 00:10:55,300
people who abuse children.

291
00:10:55,300 --> 00:10:56,860
And they say, well, you can't prove it.

292
00:10:56,860 --> 00:10:59,100
Here's another bag of money that lets us fire them

293
00:10:59,100 --> 00:11:00,620
without being able to prove it.

294
00:11:01,620 --> 00:11:03,900
Because, do you wanna run a business

295
00:11:03,900 --> 00:11:05,500
where you have to prove everything you do

296
00:11:05,500 --> 00:11:06,940
in a court of law?

297
00:11:06,940 --> 00:11:08,500
That's a terrible system.

298
00:11:08,500 --> 00:11:11,460
So, if the default is, that's what you have to do,

299
00:11:11,460 --> 00:11:14,460
you wanna go and buy your way out of that system.

300
00:11:14,460 --> 00:11:16,540
Just like, if we had to prove before a judge

301
00:11:16,540 --> 00:11:18,340
that the guy who's blocking the door

302
00:11:18,340 --> 00:11:19,700
shouldn't be blocking the door,

303
00:11:19,700 --> 00:11:22,940
we might all be dead before the case gets resolved.

304
00:11:22,940 --> 00:11:25,220
So, we don't wanna have to prove it.

305
00:11:25,220 --> 00:11:28,180
We just wanna say, yeah, whatever, shut up,

306
00:11:28,180 --> 00:11:30,740
you're gone, that's it, end of story, the end.

307
00:11:30,740 --> 00:11:33,420
Right, to fast forward to the resolution,

308
00:11:33,420 --> 00:11:35,420
which, doesn't that open up the possibility

309
00:11:35,420 --> 00:11:37,900
that you might once in a while fire an innocent teacher?

310
00:11:37,900 --> 00:11:41,820
Yes, and of course, to compensate for that possibility,

311
00:11:41,820 --> 00:11:43,460
what's the efficient thing to do?

312
00:11:44,460 --> 00:11:45,500
Another bag of money.

313
00:11:46,820 --> 00:11:49,260
So, every objection, if the objection is,

314
00:11:49,260 --> 00:11:51,420
yeah, but we're worried that a very small number of people

315
00:11:51,420 --> 00:11:53,140
might suffer harm.

316
00:11:53,140 --> 00:11:55,180
Reasonable, bag of money, shut up.

317
00:11:55,180 --> 00:11:57,900
Another bag of money, shut up.

318
00:11:57,900 --> 00:12:00,500
All right, and now, our business is done.

319
00:12:02,220 --> 00:12:03,140
So, very simple.

320
00:12:04,380 --> 00:12:07,380
Okay, so, questions about anything in Whitman?

321
00:12:08,660 --> 00:12:11,180
So, and again, if you wanna start thinking about

322
00:12:11,180 --> 00:12:14,020
how Whitman would rationalize any policy,

323
00:12:14,020 --> 00:12:16,940
again, normally, of course,

324
00:12:16,940 --> 00:12:18,340
so he's gotta say the status quo

325
00:12:18,340 --> 00:12:19,740
is actually counter-exfficient,

326
00:12:19,740 --> 00:12:21,260
with, again, rare exceptions,

327
00:12:21,260 --> 00:12:24,340
or there could be like a short-run disequilibrium,

328
00:12:24,340 --> 00:12:26,620
but again, wants to say the status quo is efficient.

329
00:12:26,620 --> 00:12:28,820
So then, for any given policy that you think of

330
00:12:28,820 --> 00:12:30,620
as actually clearly inefficient,

331
00:12:30,620 --> 00:12:33,820
he needs to have a story about why it actually,

332
00:12:33,820 --> 00:12:36,540
when you really, fully understand what's going on,

333
00:12:36,540 --> 00:12:38,180
government policy is a sensible response

334
00:12:38,180 --> 00:12:40,580
to market failure or something like that.

335
00:12:40,580 --> 00:12:43,300
And again, Whitman explicitly doesn't wanna say,

336
00:12:43,300 --> 00:12:45,300
look, this is a terrible policy,

337
00:12:45,300 --> 00:12:50,300
but because there's no way of bribing everyone

338
00:12:50,300 --> 00:12:52,780
to make them all like it, there's nothing better,

339
00:12:52,780 --> 00:12:54,700
because again, that would be the Pareto standard.

340
00:12:54,700 --> 00:12:56,380
Whitman doesn't wanna defend anything

341
00:12:56,380 --> 00:12:57,820
by the Pareto standard,

342
00:12:57,820 --> 00:12:59,980
wants to defend it by the counter-extended.

343
00:12:59,980 --> 00:13:02,940
So, right, defending things by the Pareto standard,

344
00:13:02,940 --> 00:13:04,140
right, saying, well, look,

345
00:13:04,140 --> 00:13:05,380
it exists because it's Pareto efficient.

346
00:13:05,380 --> 00:13:09,420
Remember, that is a clear case of damning through praise,

347
00:13:09,420 --> 00:13:12,420
because remember, what in the world is Pareto efficient?

348
00:13:12,420 --> 00:13:13,260
The real world?

349
00:13:13,260 --> 00:13:17,140
Everything, everything's Pareto efficient.

350
00:13:17,140 --> 00:13:19,140
Syrian Civil War is Pareto efficient.

351
00:13:19,140 --> 00:13:20,300
How can you say such a horrible thing?

352
00:13:20,300 --> 00:13:23,260
Well, who's gonna lose if the Syrian Civil War gets ended?

353
00:13:24,380 --> 00:13:26,180
Yeah, well, I don't know, maybe he wins,

354
00:13:26,180 --> 00:13:29,380
but arms dealers, arms dealers are better off

355
00:13:29,380 --> 00:13:31,260
from the Syrian Civil War, right?

356
00:13:31,260 --> 00:13:36,260
So, and then how about just sadists, misanthropes,

357
00:13:36,540 --> 00:13:38,660
people who just want Arabs to die,

358
00:13:38,660 --> 00:13:40,540
and like, just get a sick thrill of watching it,

359
00:13:40,540 --> 00:13:41,380
they're worse off.

360
00:13:41,380 --> 00:13:42,660
Does anyone compensate them and say,

361
00:13:42,660 --> 00:13:45,300
well, dear, sick, hateful person,

362
00:13:45,300 --> 00:13:47,620
here's a $100 check to compensate you

363
00:13:47,620 --> 00:13:49,980
for the tragic early end of this war.

364
00:13:49,980 --> 00:13:51,820
No, we don't do any of those things.

365
00:13:51,820 --> 00:13:55,100
And, you know, and that would be what it would take

366
00:13:55,100 --> 00:13:57,300
for ending the war to be a Pareto improvement.

367
00:13:57,300 --> 00:13:58,620
But for a Calrix improvement,

368
00:13:58,620 --> 00:14:00,820
none of that stuff needs to be done, right?

369
00:14:00,820 --> 00:14:03,780
We could just go and like, okay, the war is over.

370
00:14:03,780 --> 00:14:04,820
That is it.

371
00:14:04,820 --> 00:14:06,780
So, these are the winners, these are the losers,

372
00:14:06,780 --> 00:14:08,580
it's all over, that's it, right?

373
00:14:08,580 --> 00:14:10,620
And that could be, would in fact,

374
00:14:10,620 --> 00:14:14,100
that would likely be the Calrix efficient, right?

375
00:14:14,100 --> 00:14:16,380
To just be clear, and again, like, there's,

376
00:14:16,380 --> 00:14:17,220
like, which ending?

377
00:14:17,220 --> 00:14:18,300
Well, there's so many endings

378
00:14:18,300 --> 00:14:21,220
that are big Calrix improvements over the status quo.

379
00:14:21,220 --> 00:14:22,220
Right, that's clear.

380
00:14:24,460 --> 00:14:28,140
All right, so, all right, so here's Whitman.

381
00:14:28,140 --> 00:14:31,500
Now, remember I was saying that,

382
00:14:33,060 --> 00:14:36,300
of course, we've got this miracle of creation here,

383
00:14:36,300 --> 00:14:37,980
which does hinge upon the assumption

384
00:14:37,980 --> 00:14:39,820
of rational expectations.

385
00:14:39,820 --> 00:14:43,260
And when Whitman complains about public choice economists

386
00:14:43,260 --> 00:14:45,020
assuming extreme voter stupidity,

387
00:14:45,020 --> 00:14:46,780
another way of putting his complaint is he's saying,

388
00:14:46,780 --> 00:14:47,860
we are assuming that voters

389
00:14:47,860 --> 00:14:50,020
don't have rational expectations.

390
00:14:50,020 --> 00:14:52,700
All right, and that's terrible, that's terrible.

391
00:14:52,700 --> 00:14:54,980
All right, now, this brings me to a question

392
00:14:54,980 --> 00:14:58,100
that weighed heavily on me after reading Whitman,

393
00:14:58,100 --> 00:15:00,980
which is, so what reasons are there to believe

394
00:15:00,980 --> 00:15:04,060
that the rational expectations assumption is true?

395
00:15:04,060 --> 00:15:07,020
Certainly to believe that it's always true.

396
00:15:07,020 --> 00:15:08,260
Okay, all right.

397
00:15:08,260 --> 00:15:10,340
Now, when I first started researching this,

398
00:15:10,340 --> 00:15:12,300
I will admit I didn't go into it with an open mind.

399
00:15:12,300 --> 00:15:13,500
It just always seemed crazy to me

400
00:15:13,500 --> 00:15:15,940
to think that voters had rational expectations.

401
00:15:15,940 --> 00:15:18,860
Right, just, the reason I got into economics

402
00:15:18,860 --> 00:15:22,060
was because I read so many things in economics

403
00:15:22,060 --> 00:15:24,260
that seemed so different from what I had heard.

404
00:15:24,260 --> 00:15:27,220
And not in, like, just like a variance reducing way,

405
00:15:27,220 --> 00:15:28,740
but in the sense that almost everybody

406
00:15:28,740 --> 00:15:30,580
I'd ever talked to said one thing,

407
00:15:30,580 --> 00:15:32,980
and economists said almost exactly the opposite.

408
00:15:34,020 --> 00:15:36,900
So, and that's why, that is my autobiography,

409
00:15:36,900 --> 00:15:39,020
why I got interested in economics.

410
00:15:39,020 --> 00:15:40,260
You know, I read some economists saying

411
00:15:40,260 --> 00:15:41,300
the minimum wage is a bad idea

412
00:15:41,300 --> 00:15:42,940
because it causes disemployment.

413
00:15:42,940 --> 00:15:44,900
And then I didn't say, huh,

414
00:15:44,900 --> 00:15:46,540
but what is this evidence that the disemployment effect

415
00:15:46,540 --> 00:15:48,940
is larger than what normal people think?

416
00:15:48,940 --> 00:15:50,260
And why didn't I say that?

417
00:15:50,260 --> 00:15:53,060
Because in my entire life, I had never, ever heard anyone

418
00:15:53,060 --> 00:15:54,460
claim there was any disemployment effect

419
00:15:54,460 --> 00:15:55,820
in the minimum wage at all.

420
00:15:57,420 --> 00:15:58,540
Right, and the normal view, rather,

421
00:15:58,540 --> 00:16:00,220
is just the higher, the better.

422
00:16:00,220 --> 00:16:02,460
And then I read some economists saying there's a downside,

423
00:16:02,460 --> 00:16:04,140
and I felt very cheated.

424
00:16:04,140 --> 00:16:07,100
Like, how could this information have been kept from me?

425
00:16:07,100 --> 00:16:09,820
All right, but anyway, so, I admit,

426
00:16:09,820 --> 00:16:11,060
when I went into the subject,

427
00:16:11,060 --> 00:16:13,660
I suspected that the rationalization assumption

428
00:16:13,660 --> 00:16:15,580
would not really, really work out.

429
00:16:15,580 --> 00:16:17,420
But I did think that I'd have to go

430
00:16:17,420 --> 00:16:20,500
and argue with the biggest empirical literature,

431
00:16:20,500 --> 00:16:21,740
but I actually found there really wasn't

432
00:16:21,740 --> 00:16:22,660
much empirical literature.

433
00:16:22,660 --> 00:16:25,100
Mostly, it was just the power of theory,

434
00:16:25,100 --> 00:16:29,420
saying, look, well, the rationalization assumption says this,

435
00:16:29,420 --> 00:16:31,740
and that's the definition of rationality,

436
00:16:31,740 --> 00:16:33,220
and economists, you're an economist,

437
00:16:33,220 --> 00:16:36,100
you don't wanna call people rational, do you?

438
00:16:36,100 --> 00:16:38,380
Maybe I do, I do.

439
00:16:38,380 --> 00:16:40,500
All right, so anyway, one of my main research ideas

440
00:16:40,500 --> 00:16:42,500
that came out of Whitman's work is to say,

441
00:16:42,500 --> 00:16:45,940
just like economists are used to thinking about

442
00:16:45,940 --> 00:16:49,300
people weighing the costs and benefits of information,

443
00:16:49,300 --> 00:16:52,540
or in other words, weighing how ignorant they wanna be,

444
00:16:52,540 --> 00:16:55,180
right, you can make a rational choice to be ignorant.

445
00:16:55,180 --> 00:16:58,060
How could it ever be rational to be ignorant?

446
00:16:58,060 --> 00:17:01,260
Well, you know this whenever you decide

447
00:17:01,260 --> 00:17:04,540
that you're not gonna spend 100% of your time studying.

448
00:17:04,540 --> 00:17:07,220
Right, spend more time studying, you would know more, right?

449
00:17:07,220 --> 00:17:10,340
But everyone here at some point is gonna say,

450
00:17:10,340 --> 00:17:13,300
I've studied enough, I know enough microeconomics,

451
00:17:13,300 --> 00:17:15,420
I'm not gonna put in any more time,

452
00:17:15,420 --> 00:17:17,820
and you are therefore choosing ignorance.

453
00:17:17,820 --> 00:17:20,140
Of course, hopefully, as PhD students,

454
00:17:20,140 --> 00:17:23,340
you achieve a very high level of knowledge,

455
00:17:23,340 --> 00:17:26,220
and then you say, I'm not gonna work any harder

456
00:17:26,220 --> 00:17:28,100
to remove the last little bit of ignorance,

457
00:17:28,100 --> 00:17:31,100
but still, you are accepting ignorance.

458
00:17:31,100 --> 00:17:33,300
Right, so just like it sometimes could be rational

459
00:17:33,300 --> 00:17:36,420
to be ignorant, to say it's not worth the value of my time

460
00:17:36,420 --> 00:17:39,300
to go and understand this material any better,

461
00:17:39,300 --> 00:17:42,460
I also think sometimes it could be rational to be irrational.

462
00:17:42,460 --> 00:17:44,860
It could be rational to deviate from full rationality

463
00:17:44,860 --> 00:17:46,620
to not have rational expectations.

464
00:17:47,660 --> 00:17:49,140
Right, or in other words, I say you can think about

465
00:17:49,140 --> 00:17:50,580
irrationalities being a normal good,

466
00:17:50,580 --> 00:17:52,700
something that people might want.

467
00:17:52,700 --> 00:17:54,780
Now, why would people want this?

468
00:17:54,780 --> 00:17:57,180
Well, there's plenty of reasons why.

469
00:17:57,180 --> 00:17:59,060
Right, so here's a really obvious one,

470
00:17:59,060 --> 00:18:02,220
which is that people get comfort, security,

471
00:18:02,220 --> 00:18:04,020
and identity from their beliefs.

472
00:18:05,700 --> 00:18:08,020
All right, so if you wanna make friends with someone,

473
00:18:08,020 --> 00:18:10,180
it's generally not a good idea to say,

474
00:18:10,180 --> 00:18:12,260
what's your religion, and then say, oh,

475
00:18:12,260 --> 00:18:14,860
well, here's all the things wrong with that religion.

476
00:18:14,860 --> 00:18:16,540
All right, so, and why not?

477
00:18:16,540 --> 00:18:17,580
Because it hurts their feelings.

478
00:18:17,580 --> 00:18:19,580
They won't like it, right?

479
00:18:19,580 --> 00:18:20,780
And so, and you say, well,

480
00:18:20,780 --> 00:18:22,980
why don't you want information here?

481
00:18:22,980 --> 00:18:25,780
Well, you're trying to deprive me of something I care,

482
00:18:25,780 --> 00:18:27,980
of beliefs that I care about, that give me meaning,

483
00:18:27,980 --> 00:18:30,100
that make my life feel important.

484
00:18:30,100 --> 00:18:31,620
Right, so I don't like that.

485
00:18:32,500 --> 00:18:34,620
So that is one big reason.

486
00:18:34,620 --> 00:18:36,220
And of course, in the modern world,

487
00:18:36,220 --> 00:18:38,060
religion has become less important,

488
00:18:38,060 --> 00:18:41,900
and politics has taken its place to a very large degree.

489
00:18:41,900 --> 00:18:43,460
So again, people get a lot of,

490
00:18:43,460 --> 00:18:47,380
a sense of being a good and right and decent person

491
00:18:47,380 --> 00:18:49,580
out of being a liberal, or being a conservative,

492
00:18:49,580 --> 00:18:51,300
or being a social justice warrior,

493
00:18:51,300 --> 00:18:53,020
or being an internet troll.

494
00:18:53,020 --> 00:18:55,460
Right, all these things make people feel

495
00:18:55,460 --> 00:18:58,020
like their life is worth living.

496
00:18:58,020 --> 00:19:00,060
Right, so when I was writing this book,

497
00:19:00,060 --> 00:19:02,540
I came across a book called The God That Failed,

498
00:19:02,540 --> 00:19:05,460
which is just a bunch of autobiographies

499
00:19:05,460 --> 00:19:07,260
of former communists.

500
00:19:07,260 --> 00:19:08,620
So every chapter is a person saying,

501
00:19:08,620 --> 00:19:11,700
I used to be a communist, now I'm not, here's what happened.

502
00:19:11,700 --> 00:19:14,780
Not one of these chapters had a former communist saying,

503
00:19:14,780 --> 00:19:17,020
and it was such a joy to learn new things

504
00:19:17,020 --> 00:19:18,660
about the eras of communism.

505
00:19:18,660 --> 00:19:20,860
Every time I learned a new thing, it was really fun,

506
00:19:20,860 --> 00:19:22,820
and I'm just a curious person, so I was saying,

507
00:19:22,820 --> 00:19:25,380
oh my God, Stalin's not the greatest person, I didn't know.

508
00:19:25,380 --> 00:19:26,500
Well, tell me more.

509
00:19:26,500 --> 00:19:29,380
Right, none of the people had that reaction.

510
00:19:29,380 --> 00:19:32,980
Instead, people said it hurt, it felt so bad,

511
00:19:32,980 --> 00:19:36,500
it felt like a deprivation of meaning in their lives,

512
00:19:36,500 --> 00:19:38,340
they just felt sick.

513
00:19:38,340 --> 00:19:40,900
Right, so one of them, I think, was Arthur Kessler.

514
00:19:40,900 --> 00:19:42,780
He said, you know, abandoning communism

515
00:19:42,780 --> 00:19:46,820
is like emotional harakiri, which is the Japanese term

516
00:19:46,820 --> 00:19:50,220
for the disrespectful Japanese term for ritual suicide.

517
00:19:50,220 --> 00:19:53,060
So he compared abandoning Stalinism

518
00:19:53,060 --> 00:19:55,300
to like putting a sword in your own stomach

519
00:19:55,300 --> 00:19:58,540
and stirring it around, and it hurts that much.

520
00:19:58,540 --> 00:20:25,540
Right, this is a very normal human reaction.


